  "components": [
    {
      "id": "lipschitz-by-design.html::exercise-15::proved-component-1",
      "statement": "For every actual finite real skew-symmetric matrix A, I+A and I-A are invertible. The actual rational transform Q=(I-A)(I+A)inv satisfies Q-transpose*Q=I and determinant one. Q+I is exactly twice the denominator inverse and is itself invertible, so Q has no nonzero eigenvector with eigenvalue -1.",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actualCayley",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_skew_cayley_denominator_gram_is_positive",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_skew_cayley_denominator_is_invertible",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_skew_cayley_numerator_is_transposed_denominator",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_skew_cayley_numerator_is_invertible",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_cayley_is_orthogonal",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_cayley_has_determinant_one",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_cayley_plus_identity_is_twice_inverse",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_cayley_plus_identity_is_invertible",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayley.actual_cayley_has_no_negative_one_eigenvector"
      ],
      "status": "approved_precise_component"
    },
    {
      "id": "lipschitz-by-design.html::exercise-15::proved-component-2",
      "statement": "For the actual two-dimensional skew matrix [[0,a],[-a,0]], the denominator inverse is [[1,-a],[a,1]]/(1+a^2), and its actual Cayley transform is [[1-a^2,-2a],[2a,1-a^2]]/(1+a^2). This is exactly the actual rotation matrix at angle2*arctan(a), with angle strictly between -pi and pi. At a=1/2 the actual matrix is [[3/5,-4/5],[4/5,3/5]]. The exact angle in degrees differs from53.13 by at most.005, proved through actual alternating arctan sums and analytic pi bounds.",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actualPlaneSkew",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actualPlaneRotation",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_plane_matrix_is_skew",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_plane_denominator_inverse",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_plane_cayley_formula",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_arctan_rotation_coordinates",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_plane_cayley_is_rotation",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_plane_cayley_angle_is_in_open_pi_interval",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_half_parameter_cayley_matrix",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actualHalfArctanTerm",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_half_arctan_terms_are_antitone",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteModulesCayleyPlane.actual_half_parameter_angle_rounds_to_53_13_degrees"
      ],
      "status": "approved_precise_component"
    },
    {
      "id": "lipschitz-by-design.html::exercise-15::proved-component-3",
      "statement": "The range of the actual scalar two-dimensional Cayley parameterization is exactly the two-dimensional orthogonal determinant-one matrices except -I. Every orthogonal determinant-minus-one matrix has an actual nonzero eigenvector of eigenvalue -1. Thus the omitted two-dimensional orthogonal matrices are precisely the pi rotation and every reflection.",
